{"id": "2026-11-25-news-north-korea-expels-swiss-envoy-shuts-office", "date": "2026-11-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "North Korea expels Swiss envoy, shuts office", "summary": "North Korea declared the head of the Swiss diplomatic office in Pyongyang persona non grata and demanded the office close over Swiss sanctions on the Songdowon camp.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/international/schweizer-buero-in-nordkorea-nordkorea-weist-schweizer-diplomaten-aus-und-schliesst-vertretung", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "geopolitics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "North Korea has declared Sylvain Astier, head of the Swiss diplomatic office in Pyongyang, persona non grata and given him 48 hours to leave, the state agency KCNA reported on Wednesday 25 November. According to KCNA, Pak Hyon Su, director-general for Europe at the North Korean foreign ministry, summoned Astier and handed him a memorandum demanding that the office close. KCNA called Switzerland's sanctions on the Songdowon international children's camp near Wonsan a \"hostile act\". The day before, Kim Yo Jong had threatened to close the office over the same sanctions. Switzerland imposed them in early November when it adopted the EU's 21st Russia package, which targets the camp for taking part in programmes that bring Ukrainian children from occupied territories there. The Federal Department of Foreign Affairs (FDFA) had said on Tuesday that neither the Swiss embassy in Beijing nor the Pyongyang office had been officially contacted. It said its sanctions policy is separate from its diplomatic presence and that the office exists to keep channels of dialogue open. The office only reopened on 19 October, after Foreign Minister Ignazio Cassis visited Pyongyang.", "why": "barely five weeks after it reopened, Switzerland is about to lose one of the few Western channels into Pyongyang, because it adopted EU sanctions.", "thread_id": "2026-11-25-news-north-korea-expels-swiss-envoy-shuts-office", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-25", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["North Korea", "Switzerland", "Sylvain Astier", "FDFA", "Songdowon"]}
{"id": "2026-11-25-news-pregnancy-care-free-of-cost-sharing-from-day-one", "date": "2026-11-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "Pregnancy care free of cost-sharing from day one", "summary": "From 1 January 2027 pregnant women are exempt from deductible and co-payment from the start of pregnancy, pharmacists can bill vaccinations, and out-of-canton reference tariffs are harmonised.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/s9YnGAWYCA0X", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "health"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "From 1 January 2027, pregnant women will pay no deductible or co-payment from the first day of pregnancy instead of from the 13th week, the Federal Council decided on Wednesday 25 November. The change is one of several measures from parliament's second cost-containment package of March 2025 that the government is now bringing into force. From the same date, midwives as well as doctors can notify the insurer that a pregnancy has begun. Pharmacists will be able to bill basic health insurance for helping patients take their medicines correctly and for certain prevention services, and to give some vaccines, including the flu jab, to people aged 16 and over without a prescription. Cantons must also change how they set \"reference tariffs\", the most that basic insurance pays when a patient is treated in a hospital outside their home canton. By 1 January 2029 at the latest, these must match the highest tariff for a comparable treatment in a hospital on the home canton's list, so choosing a hospital elsewhere should no longer leave patients with extra costs. The communiqué does not estimate what the measures will cost or save.", "why": "early pregnancy is when costs often land on the deductible, and a flu jab at the pharmacy paid by basic insurance changes everyday access to care from January.", "thread_id": "2026-11-25-news-pregnancy-care-free-of-cost-sharing-from-day-one", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-25", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Council", "LAMal"]}
{"id": "2026-11-25-news-bern-proposes-one-flat-work-expense-deduction", "date": "2026-11-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "Bern proposes one flat work-expense deduction", "summary": "The Federal Council adopted a bill replacing itemised work-expense deductions, including meals, with a single flat deduction while keeping commuting deductions separate.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/eVbpG5w4C0mF", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Federal Council sent parliament a bill on Wednesday 25 November that would replace most itemised work-expense tax deductions for employees with a single flat deduction. Commuting costs and the cost of staying away from home during the week would still be deducted separately. Everything else, including meals eaten away from home, would go into one flat amount that needs no receipts, is the same whether people work at home or in the office, and is reduced only for those who work part of the year. Taxpayers could no longer deduct their actual costs instead. The Finance Department would set the federal amount so that the reform costs the federal budget as little as possible, and each canton would set its own amount. The government had wanted to fold commuting costs into the flat deduction too, but dropped this after most cantons opposed it in consultation.", "why": "everyone who works from home gains the meal allowance, but anyone whose actual work costs are higher than the flat amount loses the right to claim them. Parliament now decides.", "thread_id": "2026-11-25-news-bern-proposes-one-flat-work-expense-deduction", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-25", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Council", "Federal Department of Finance"]}
{"id": "2026-11-25-news-solar-subsidies-rise-from-january", "date": "2026-11-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "Solar subsidies rise from January", "summary": "The Federal Council raised one-off payments for small and mid-sized photovoltaic installations and revised several energy ordinances effective 1 January 2027.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/5aKhPwpNDa3M", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The Federal Council raised the one-off subsidy for small and mid-sized solar installations on Wednesday 25 November, as part of a set of revised energy ordinances that take effect on 1 January 2027. The capacity-based payment rises by CHF 40 per kilowatt both for installations under 30 kW and for those from 30 to 100 kW. The higher one-off payment for installations under 150 kW that do not use their own power is now CHF 550 per kW. The same package cuts investment grants for biomass plants while raising their operating grants, ends federal support for solar thermal heat for industry, and simplifies planning for high-voltage lines, so that the government sets the route corridor and the choice between overhead line and cable directly. It also changes how nuclear operators' contribution to federal liability cover is calculated.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-25-news-solar-subsidies-rise-from-january", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-25", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Council"]}
{"id": "2026-11-25-news-non-eu-work-permit-quotas-held-for-2027", "date": "2026-11-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "Non-EU work permit quotas held for 2027", "summary": "The Federal Council kept 2027 quotas for third-country skilled workers at 8,500 and left EU/EFTA service-provider and UK quotas unchanged.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/dvHGtzzJ-yR0", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The Federal Council kept quotas for skilled workers from outside the EU and EFTA (the European Free Trade Association) unchanged for 2027, at 8,500 permits, on Wednesday 25 November. That is 4,500 residence (B) permits and 4,000 short-term (L) permits. The 3,500 permits for EU/EFTA service providers working in Switzerland more than 120 days a year and the 3,500 for UK citizens also stay the same. Companies used only 69% of the third-country quota in 2025, down from 74% a year earlier. The government pointed to an unemployment rate of 3% in September 2026, up 0.2 points on a year earlier.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-25-news-non-eu-work-permit-quotas-held-for-2027", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-25", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Council", "EFTA"]}
{"id": "2026-11-25-news-efta-thailand-trade-deal-starts-in-january", "date": "2026-11-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "EFTA–Thailand trade deal starts in January", "summary": "The EFTA–Thailand free trade agreement enters into force on 1 January 2027, giving tariff relief to 99.7% of current Swiss exports to Thailand.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/oeulI8jYdKFU", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The free trade agreement between the EFTA states and Thailand will take effect on 1 January 2027, the Federal Council said on Wednesday 25 November. It adopted the ordinance changes needed to apply the agreed tariff cuts. Bern says 99.7% of current Swiss exports to Thailand will get tariff relief, some only after transition periods. Trade in goods between the two countries, excluding precious metals, was about CHF 2 billion in 2025. The deal was signed in Davos on 23 January 2025 and approved by parliament on 20 March 2026.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-25-news-efta-thailand-trade-deal-starts-in-january", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-25", "event_date": "2027-01-01", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["EFTA", "Thailand"]}
{"id": "2026-11-25-news-rights-court-sets-rules-for-weaponised-migration", "date": "2026-11-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "Rights court sets rules for weaponised migration", "summary": "The ECHR Grand Chamber ruled that removals to an instrumentalising state are not in themselves an Article 3 violation but states must identify vulnerable people first.", "url": "https://hudoc.echr.coe.int/eng-press?i=003-8704399-12426091", "source_domain": "hudoc.echr.coe.int", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Grand Chamber of the European Court of Human Rights ruled on Tuesday 24 November that when a hostile state deliberately sends migrants across a border, returning them to that state is not in itself a violation of the ban on inhuman treatment, but border authorities must first identify people who are especially at risk. The three cases involved Afghans, Iraqi Kurds and Cubans who tried to enter Poland, Latvia and Lithuania from Belarus in 2021–22. The court used the term \"instrumentalisation of migration\" for this tactic of pushing migrants towards a neighbour's border to destabilise it. It said states may restrict entry or even close their borders entirely, and that they need not check whether migrants would have access to asylum in Belarus. Before returning anyone, however, they must screen for two groups: people with evident vulnerabilities (age, illness, disability) and people with a specific basis to claim a real and immediate risk of ill-treatment. The court unanimously found violations for one Afghan applicant whom Poland removed to Belarus on 20 October 2021 despite an interim court order, and for the children and their accompanying families in the Latvian case. It found no violation for the Latvian adults and, by 11 votes to 6, none in the Lithuanian case.", "why": "this is the first time Europe's top human-rights court has set out the rules for \"weaponised\" migration, and the standard applies in all 46 Council of Europe states, Switzerland among them.", "thread_id": "2026-11-25-news-rights-court-sets-rules-for-weaponised-migration", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-25", "event_date": "2026-11-24", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["European Court of Human Rights", "Poland", "Latvia", "Lithuania", "Belarus"]}
{"id": "2026-11-25-news-reports-russian-nuclear-satellite-within-months", "date": "2026-11-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "Reports: Russian nuclear satellite within months", "summary": "German media citing Western intelligence report that Russia could put a nuclear-armed satellite into orbit within two months; Paris deems the risk serious and the Kremlin dismisses it.", "url": "https://www.lemonde.fr/international/article/2026/11/25/envoi-d-une-arme-nucleaire-russe-dans-l-espace-d-ici-deux-mois-un-risque-juge-serieux_6815074_3210.html", "source_domain": "lemonde.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["disputed"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "security"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Several German media outlets reported on Tuesday 24 November that Western intelligence believes Russia could place a satellite carrying a nuclear warhead in orbit within about two months. Le Monde reports that Paris considers the risk serious. Citing work by intelligence services and Western military officials, including Germany's armed forces and its foreign intelligence agency (BND), the reports say the satellite, named \"Sputnik-S\", has already been built and is stored near Moscow. The outlets did not name specific sources. Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said that because of this it was \"hardly worth taking them seriously\". Washington has warned since 2024 that Moscow is working on such a weapon. What is new is the reported launch timeline. The 1967 Outer Space Treaty, which Russia has signed, bans placing nuclear weapons in orbit. Antje Nötzold, a space-security researcher at the Bundeswehr University Munich, told SRF that a nuclear explosion in orbit would immediately destroy nearby satellites and leave radiation that could disable others for months, cutting off satellite communications and the positioning and timing signals that transport, power grids and payments rely on.", "why": "the claim is unconfirmed and comes from anonymous sourcing, but it is the first reported date for a move that would break a 60-year ban on nuclear weapons in orbit.", "thread_id": "2026-11-25-news-reports-russian-nuclear-satellite-within-months", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-25", "event_date": "2026-11-24", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Russia", "Outer Space Treaty", "BND", "Kremlin"]}
{"id": "2026-11-25-news-houthi-drones-target-saudi-oil-terminal", "date": "2026-11-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "Houthi drones target Saudi oil terminal", "summary": "Saudi air defences intercepted Houthi drones over Ras Tanura after a Houthi drone struck Riyadh's tallest tower, injuring 11.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/25/saudi-led-coalition-intercepts-houthi-attacks-after-hodeidah-strike", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Saudi air defences destroyed four Houthi drones over the Ras Tanura oil refinery and export terminal on Wednesday 25 November, the day after a Houthi drone hit Riyadh's tallest tower and injured 11 people, Saudi officials said. Coalition spokesman Turki al-Maliki said falling fragments caused no casualties in an initial assessment. Houthi spokesman Yahya Saree claimed the strikes on Ras Tanura had started fires, and that a three-phase attack had hit Riyadh's financial district. The tower, which houses the Saudi sovereign wealth fund, was first struck on Thursday 19 November. Tuesday's attack followed coalition air strikes on the Red Sea port of Hodeidah, the main entry point for food into Houthi-held northern Yemen. The Houthis told workers and residents near Saudi Red Sea ports, including Jeddah, to evacuate. Qatar and Pakistan, which are mediating between Iran and the United States, condemned the strike on the tower.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-25-news-houthi-drones-target-saudi-oil-terminal", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-25", "event_date": "2026-11-25", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Houthis", "Saudi Arabia", "Ras Tanura", "Riyadh", "Hodeidah"]}
{"id": "2026-11-25-news-us-jury-convicts-huawei-on-sanctions-theft", "date": "2026-11-25", "stream": "news", "headline": "US jury convicts Huawei on sanctions, theft", "summary": "A Brooklyn federal jury convicted Huawei and three subsidiaries of Iran sanctions violations, bank fraud, money laundering and trade-secret conspiracy.", "url": "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/chinese-tech-giant-huawei-and-subsidiaries-convicted-sanctions-violations-fraud-conspiracy", "source_domain": "justice.gov", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "tech"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "A federal jury in Brooklyn convicted Huawei and three of its subsidiaries on Tuesday 24 November of violating US sanctions on Iran, bank fraud, money laundering and conspiring to steal trade secrets, after a three-month trial. DW reports that the jury found the company guilty on 10 of 12 counts and acquitted it of racketeering conspiracy and of conspiring to obstruct justice. According to the Justice Department, Huawei ran its Iran business through a subsidiary called Skycom, which it falsely described to banks as an independent partner, and supplied Iran with network infrastructure and with tools to monitor and censor internet users. The department said the victims of the trade-secret thefts included Cisco, Motorola Solutions, T-Mobile and Fujitsu. These are the prosecution's descriptions of what was proven at trial. No sentence has been set, and Huawei's response was not available.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-25-news-us-jury-convicts-huawei-on-sanctions-theft", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-25", "event_date": "2026-11-24", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Huawei", "US Department of Justice", "Iran", "Skycom"]}
